  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures cross-lingual representational alignment with spoken translation retrieval, using a pronunciation-controlled dataset and comparing models trained with and without a speech translation objective. This is a different method and setting from the original sources.",
    "gate_note": "The retrieval results bear on cross-lingual alignment and how it changes when phonetic cues or a training objective are changed. The early-exit ASR error rates are not rows because they measure transcription performance, not alignment. The study does not test model scaling, capability-alignment trends, alternative similarity metrics, or mappings between representations. Retrieval curves are shown in figures; exact plotted values are not stated in the text.",
    "test_reason": "The study does not test the claim. It measures cross-lingual alignment inside one speech encoder: whether utterances in different languages retrieve their translations via SeqSim within Whisper-large-v2 or OWSM. None of the versions name this construct. It does not compare the representations of independently trained models (V1), vision and language models (V2), seeds (V3), maps between models (V4) or shared features across models (V5). Its phonetic-cue control and objective comparison concern within-model cross-lingual retrieval, not measured convergence between models, so they do not bear on V6 or V7 either. The reader's 'tests'/T2 and its V6/V7 links are rejected. The finding is recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The paraphrase is accurate. Retrieval persists without phonetic cues, mainly in final layers, and is strongly driven by the speech translation objective, arising weakly from ASR alone. Early exiting improves ASR error rates on 5 of 7 low-resource languages.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses SeqSim spoken-translation retrieval in Whisper-style speech encoders, a pronunciation-controlled challenge set, and a comparison of models with and without a speech translation objective; it does not test the original sources' scale-based claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Retrieval changes with input construction: the pronunciation-controlled challenge set shows noticeable drops relative to the full set. Retrieval is also stronger in the model trained with a speech translation objective than in the comparable model without it.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Whisper-large-v2's final encoder layers, cross-lingual spoken translation retrieval stays well above chance after pronunciation cues are removed, with noticeable drops. In OWSM, the speech translation objective strongly increases this retrieval; ASR-only training gives weak but non-trivial retrieval.",
        "proposed_claim": "Within a single multilingual speech encoder, utterances in different languages share a semantic representation space, which is driven mainly by a translation objective.",
